\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{4}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |\n| $2$ | {{field-3}} |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nThe first three sample slopes, evaluated at the initial point and first intermediate points, are already computed. Now let's find the others.\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+1,\\,-1+\\frac{13}{4}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(2,\\frac{9}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{′} & =2{x}_{3}+{y}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{4}$ | $\\frac{25}{4}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(1+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{13}{4}+\\frac{25}{4})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{75}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{4}$ | $\\frac{25}{4}$ | $\\frac{25}{8}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{25}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{8}+\\frac{25}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{4}$ | $\\frac{25}{4}$ | $\\frac{25}{8}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{17}{8}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2)≈\\frac{17}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-339800/field-1" "q-339800/field-2" "q-339800/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43ab4b4b-4462-50e5-ad14-fb31a502be7d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339800"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339822/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "059febd9-acfd-5f59-924a-501dc989d0d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339822/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "45e702b2-acbe-5463-9227-a3f20bfdde26" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339822/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b05