ld/key "field-6" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-339820/field-6/25433b7d070489a7e06715c4fcc46aff6d8ea217b3e999e2ffa3fb4e69e765f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-339820/field-6/d278d4936eb1c69dc660a77f5f1c7b1eb5b4a4db4aee8a8ad2dc52aa3f827f2e" "q-339820/field-6/25433b7d070489a7e06715c4fcc46aff6d8ea217b3e999e2ffa3fb4e69e765f9" "q-339820/field-6/413af0155897fc34ae59481611e9dd9b1167fa113bb61d909d6f597af5f038ab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339820" :question/id #uuid "7638c20e-c38c-52a7-a683-ee93846559db" :question/math-academy-id "q-339820" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-4y,\\,y(1)=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method. Complete the table below, given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | {{field-6}} |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta Method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nNow let's find the required values.\n\n- First, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-4y \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ |  |  |  |  |\n\n- The first intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{8},\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′} & =2{x}_{1}-4{y}_{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{9}{8}-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  |  |\n\n- The second intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{8},\\frac{17}{32}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & =2{x}_{2}-4{y}_{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{9}{8}-4\\cdot \\frac{17}{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |  |  |\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{17}{32}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{′} & =2{x}_{3}-4{y}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}-4\\cdot \\frac{17}{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(0+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{3}{8})\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot \\frac{9}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{3}{64}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{64} \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{3}{64}$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{35}{64}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\,(\\frac{5}{4})≈\\frac{35}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-339820/field-1" "q-339820/field-2" "q-339820/field-3" "q-339820/field-4" "q-339820/field-5" "q-339820/field-6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43ab4b4b-4462-50e5-ad14-fb31a502be7d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339820"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339801/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "6b0f0ec1-ca76-5d11-9b7c-ecee81af2fe3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339801/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "874532b4-57e4-5301-ad3f-13f429bee225" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339801/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "1d2e73cc-97b2-59ac-a958-95a447211146" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339801/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "495825c2-e834-559e-a90a-52507a38139d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-339801/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-339801/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-339801/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-339801/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339801/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "a51cb463-c477-5b6b-85e1-0ba91b979c59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [